@startuml Basic Builder Pattern

' Define the styles
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam classFontStyle bold
skinparam classBackgroundColor #f8f9fa
skinparam classBorderColor #dee2e6
skinparam arrowColor #6c757d
skinparam stereotypeCBackgroundColor #e9ecef

' Title
title Basic Builder Pattern - Core Structure

' Product
class Product <<Product>> {
  -partA: String
  -partB: String
  -partC: String
  +setPartA(part: String): void
  +setPartB(part: String): void
  +setPartC(part: String): void
  +show(): void
}

' Builder Interface
interface Builder <<interface>> {
  +buildPartA(): Builder
  +buildPartB(): Builder
  +buildPartC(): Builder
  +build(): Product
}

' Concrete Builder
class ConcreteBuilder <<ConcreteBuilder>> {
  -product: Product
  +ConcreteBuilder()
  +buildPartA(): Builder
  +buildPartB(): Builder
  +buildPartC(): Builder
  +build(): Product
}

' Director (Optional)
class Director <<Director>> {
  -builder: Builder
  +Director(builder: Builder)
  +construct(): Product
  +constructMinimal(): Product
}

' Relationships
Builder <|.. ConcreteBuilder : implements
ConcreteBuilder ..> Product : creates
Director o--> "1" Builder : uses
Director ..> Product : returns

' Pattern component notes
note right of Product
  <b>Product</b>
  The complex object being built.

  Contains multiple parts that
  are set during construction.

  <b>Characteristics:</b>
  • May have many attributes
  • Complex initialization logic
  • May have required and optional parts
end note

note left of Builder
  <b>Builder Interface</b>
  Declares construction methods
  for building parts of Product.

  <b>Key Features:</b>
  • Each build method returns Builder
    (for method chaining)
  • build() returns final Product
  • Provides fluent interface

  <b>Fluent Interface Example:</b>
  builder.buildPartA()
         .buildPartB()
         .build();
end note

note bottom of ConcreteBuilder
  <b>Concrete Builder</b>
  Implements Builder interface to
  construct and assemble parts.

  <b>Responsibilities:</b>
  • Creates new Product instance
  • Implements building steps
  • Keeps track of product being built
  • Returns completed Product

  <b>Key Method:</b>
  build() finalizes and returns Product
end note

note left of Director
  <b>Director (Optional)</b>
  Defines order in which to call
  building steps.

  <b>Purpose:</b>
  • Encapsulates construction logic
  • Reuses common construction sequences
  • Hides complexity from client
  • Creates different representations

  <b>Example:</b>
  construct() - builds full product
  constructMinimal() - builds basic version

  <b>Note:</b> Director is optional; clients
  can build directly using Builder.
end note

' Legend positioned to the right
note right of ConcreteBuilder
  <b>Builder Pattern Components:</b>

  <b>Product:</b> Complex object being constructed.
  May have many configuration options.

  <b>Builder:</b> Abstract interface for creating
  parts of Product object. Returns self to
  enable method chaining (fluent interface).

  <b>ConcreteBuilder:</b> Implements Builder interface.
  Constructs and assembles parts of Product.
  Provides method to retrieve built product.

  <b>Director (Optional):</b> Constructs object using
  Builder interface. Defines construction sequences.

  <b>Key Benefits:</b>
  • Separates construction from representation
  • Allows step-by-step construction
  • Supports different representations
  • Provides control over construction process
  • Creates immutable objects cleanly
  • Improves code readability with fluent API

  <b>Intent:</b>
  Separate the construction of a complex
  object from its representation so that
  the same construction process can create
  different representations.

  <b>When to Use:</b>
  • Object has many optional parameters
  • Construction requires multiple steps
  • Need different representations
  • Want to avoid telescoping constructors
end note

@enduml